WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:06.206
A government minister has warned that parents could lose benefits, be fined, or even

00:06.206 --> 00:11.546
face prison time if their children commit crimes, as ministers toughen the youth

00:11.546 --> 00:12.547
justice system.

00:13.447 --> 00:19.127
Parenting Orders already let courts mandate counselling or fines for parents whose

00:19.127 --> 00:24.600
children offend. Under the reforms, prison would apply only in the most extreme

00:24.600 --> 00:26.332
cases, decided by judges.

00:27.232 --> 00:32.892
Youth justice minister Jake Richards says the system should hold parents to account,

00:32.892 --> 00:37.003
arguing greater involvement comes with real responsibilities.

00:37.903 --> 00:43.014
But not everyone agrees. While ministers pilot new intervention courts and back

00:43.014 --> 00:48.449
at-risk children with funding, academics warn fining poorer families could push them

00:48.449 --> 00:49.743
deeper into poverty.

00:50.643 --> 00:56.905
Use of Parenting Orders has collapsed, from over a thousand issued in 2009/10 to

00:56.905 --> 01:01.288
just thirty three last year, prompting ministers to act.

01:02.188 --> 01:08.030
Orders can last up to a year, with breaches punishable by fines up to one thousand

01:08.030 --> 01:13.444
pounds. As the plans develop, the debate over fairness versus accountability

01:13.444 --> 01:14.156
continues.
